---
title: Steak with pan sauce
description: Lesson 1.3. Searing, the Maillard reaction step by step, and a sauce from what the sear leaves in the pan.
servings: 2
time: 30 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, heat-in-a-pan]
lesson: "1.3"
technique: searing and deglazing
status: draft
---

> Technique: searing, then deglazing. This pan starts hot, the opposite
> of lesson 1.1. The steak browns fast and leaves a brown layer stuck to
> the pan. That layer is called fond, and the sauce is made from it.
-- source: "Pan Sauces 101", America's Test Kitchen / Cook's Illustrated, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/cooksillustrated/how_tos/5551-pan-sauces-101

Pat @sirloin steaks{2}(about 250 g each, 2.5 cm thick) dry with kitchen paper and season both sides with @salt{1%tsp}.

Heat @oil{1%tbsp}(sunflower or rapeseed) in a #frying pan{}(stainless steel or cast iron, not non-stick) over high heat until the oil just starts to smoke.

> Science: browning wants heat and a dry surface. McGee puts the
> caramelisation of table sugar at about 165 °C and Maillard browning
> perhaps 50 °C below that, while water stays at 100 °C until it has
> vaporised: that is why the steak is patted dry. The reaction is
> fastest at a water activity of about 0.6 to 0.7; with more free
> water than that the reactants are diluted and it slows. In one set
> of measurements refined sunflower oil smoked at 230 to 234 °C, so oil
> that has just started to smoke means a pan well past what browning
> needs. Look up: water activity, caramelization, smoke point.
-- source: Harold McGee, On Food and Cooking, 2nd ed., Scribner, 2004, sections "The Maillard Reactions" and "High Temperatures and Dry Cooking Methods", https://app.ckbk.com/reference/food00011c14s001e001se003/high-temperatures-and-dry-cooking-methods ; El Hosry et al., "Maillard Reaction: Mechanism, Influencing Parameters, Advantages, Disadvantages, and Food Industrial Applications: A Review", Foods 14(11):1881, 2025, https://doi.org/10.3390/foods14111881 (water activity, section 3.4) ; Holgado et al., "Performance of olive-pomace oils in discontinuous and continuous frying", Foods 10(12):3081, 2021, https://doi.org/10.3390/foods10123081 (smoke points)

> Why this pan: a non-stick pan does not build up fond the way a plain
> steel or iron pan does, so it gives a weaker sauce.

Lay the steaks in the pan and cook for ~{3%minutes} a side, turning once, until both sides are deep brown and the centre reads 63 °C on a #thermometer{}.

> Science: the crust is the Maillard reaction, described by Louis
> Camille Maillard in 1912. The carbonyl group of a reducing sugar
> condenses with the amino group of an amino acid into a Schiff base,
> which rearranges into an Amadori product. That breaks down into
> reactive dicarbonyls, which degrade more amino acids (the Strecker
> degradation) into aroma compounds: pyrazines (roasted, nutty), furans
> (caramel-like) and sulfur compounds such as thiophenes and thiazoles
> (roasted, meaty). The end products are melanoidins, large brown
> molecules. Caramelisation is a different reaction: sugar alone, with
> no amino acid. Look up: Maillard reaction, reducing sugar, Schiff
> base, Amadori rearrangement, Strecker degradation, pyrazines,
> melanoidins.
-- source: El Hosry et al., "Maillard Reaction: Mechanism, Influencing Parameters, Advantages, Disadvantages, and Food Industrial Applications: A Review", Foods 14(11):1881, 2025, https://doi.org/10.3390/foods14111881 (sections 1, 2 and 4) ; McGee, "The Maillard Reactions" (caramelisation is sugar alone)

Move the steaks to a plate and leave them to rest for ~{5%minutes} while you make the sauce.

> Why: a steak keeps cooking after it leaves the pan and climbs a few
> more degrees, so the temperature you read in the pan is not where it
> ends up. The old reason for resting, that juices soak back in, is weak:
> the measured difference in lost juice is small and disputed.
-- source: Meathead Goldwyn with Greg Blonder, "The Science of Resting Meat", AmazingRibs.com, https://amazingribs.com/more-technique-and-science/more-cooking-science/science-of-resting-meat/

> Science: heat sets muscle proteins in overlapping bands: myosin at
> about 40 to 60 °C, actin at about 70 to 80 °C. As they set they
> contract and squeeze water out. A steak taken to 63 °C, plus a few
> degrees of carryover, has set its myosin and stays below the actin
> band. Look up: protein denaturation, myosin, actin, carryover
> cooking.
-- source: Jiang et al., "Molecular mechanisms underlying sensory and chemical changes in muscle foods induced by sous-vide cooking: a review", Foods 14(17):2967, 2025, https://doi.org/10.3390/foods14172967 (an older calorimetry study of beef puts actin lower, at 66 to 73 °C) ; Meathead Goldwyn with Greg Blonder, "The Science of Resting Meat", AmazingRibs.com, https://amazingribs.com/more-technique-and-science/more-cooking-science/science-of-resting-meat/ (contraction and water loss)

> Safety: the USDA's safe minimum for steak is 63 °C, read before it
> leaves the heat, then a rest of at least 3 minutes. This recipe
> follows it. Medium-rare, about 55 °C, is below that figure; many
> cooks choose it anyway.
-- source: USDA Food Safety and Inspection Service, "Safe Minimum Internal Temperature Chart", last updated 2025-04-14: steaks 145 °F (62.8 °C), measured before removing from the heat, and rest at least 3 minutes, https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/safe-temperature-chart

Pour off all but a thin film of fat from the pan, keeping the brown layer. Set the pan over medium heat, add @shallot{1}(finely chopped) and stir for ~{1%minute}, until soft.

Pour in @red wine{75%ml} and @beef stock{150%ml}. Scrape the bottom of the pan with a #wooden spoon{} until the brown layer has dissolved into the liquid and the pan bottom is clean. Simmer for ~{5%minutes}, until the liquid has reduced by about half and coats the back of the spoon.

Take the pan off the heat. Stir in @cold butter{30%g}(cut into 3 pieces) one piece at a time, then pour in any juice from the steak plate. Taste and add salt if it needs it.

Slice the steaks across the grain and spoon the sauce over.

> Next time, without the recipe: sear anything in a plain steel pan,
> then look at what is stuck to it. Soften something small and sharp
> (shallot, garlic), add a liquid, scrape, boil it down, and finish off
> the heat with cold butter.
